call mtr.add_suppression("Doublewrite (load|open or create) file .* size .* is not a multiple of the configured page size");
call mtr.add_suppression("Setting thread .* nice to 0 failed");
call mtr.add_suppression("Failed to set (I/O thread|purge coordinator thread) priority");
#
# Set up ~32 separate tablespaces. buf_pool_get() (buf0buf.ic) picks
# the buffer pool instance for a page by hashing (space_id, page_no
# >> 6), not by round-robin counter - so the instance spread below
# comes from having many distinct space_ids, not from many pages per
# table.
#
#
# Scenario 1: with the cap skipped (guard branch forced by debug
# point), an explicit instance count above the old ceiling (64) and
# above any real host's physical core count is honored as-is, and
# pages from the 32 tablespaces reach instance IDs well beyond 63 -
# i.e. the raised ceiling (256) is not just a compile-time constant,
# it is functionally reachable.
#
# restart: --debug=d,bp_topology_unavailable --innodb-buffer-pool-instances=200 --innodb-buffer-pool-size=1G
include/wait_for_pattern_in_file.inc [CPU topology unavailable, buffer pool instance cap skipped]
guard-branch instance count matches the request: 200
# Guard branch: topology snapshot forced unavailable -> cap skipped,
# a diagnostic line is printed instead (SYSTEM level, visible at the
# default log-error-verbosity), and the instance count above is
# exactly the requested value, not reduced.
include/assert_grep.inc [topology-unavailable diagnostic line is present exactly once]
# Pages from 32 distinct tablespaces are expected to reach instance
# IDs at or above half of the requested count (verified analytically
# for this test's actual space_id range when it was written, for
# several different starting space_id offsets - not a tight
# guarantee for an arbitrary space_id set, but the probability of a
# false negative here, if that analysis were ever wrong for a
# different space_id range, is itself astronomically small).
pool id spread reaches the upper half of the instance range
#
# Scenario 2: explicit instance count above the physical core count,
# topology available (no debug point) - the cap fires, reducing it
# to this host's actual physical core count, with a log line naming
# old/new/core-count, and the effective sysvar is self-consistent
# with what the log line says. Neither number is assumed here - both
# come from the server's own output.
#
# restart: --innodb-buffer-pool-instances=200 --innodb-buffer-pool-size=1G
actual_instances read back from the server after requesting 200 instances
PASS: capped-instances log line is self-consistent with the effective sysvar
#
# Scenario 3: default start is unaffected by this unit - same
# instance count across two consecutive plain-default restarts (not
# compared against scenario 2's explicit-request-capped state, which
# is a different configuration entirely), and no capped-instances
# line appears in the log (there is at most one, from scenario 2,
# for the whole test - checked below).
#
# restart:
# restart:
default instance count unchanged across restart: 1
include/assert_grep.inc [capped-instances line occurs at most once (from scenario 2, if the cap fired there) after a default-config restart]
